// errcheck exclude list for the `ww` CLI.
//
// Purpose: silence the established CLI-output convention so that `errcheck`
// surfaces the err returns that actually carry recoverable information
// instead of the high-volume noise floor of `fmt.Fprint*` calls.
//
// What's listed below: the `fmt.Fprint*` family. Every flagged call site in
// this module that uses these helpers writes informational / diagnostic /
// table-row output to a user-facing writer (`os.Stdout`, `os.Stderr`, a
// cobra command's `OutOrStdout()` / `ErrOrStderr()`, a `tabwriter.Writer`,
// or a per-command captured `opts.Out`). In a one-shot CLI:
//   - a broken-pipe / failed-write to stdout has no actionable recovery —
//     the process is about to exit and the user has already lost the
//     downstream consumer that closed the pipe;
//   - explicitly threading the err through every print site triples the
//     line count of every formatting helper and obscures the real
//     business logic;
//   - the established convention across all of `cmd/*.go` +
//     `internal/workspace/*.go` is to discard the return — wrapping a
//     handful of sites would just make the codebase inconsistent.
//
// What's NOT listed: `*.Close()` and `*.Body.Close()` patterns. Those
// remain enforced. Close-side errors on write paths can lose buffered
// data, and HTTP body closes are sometimes genuine cleanup bugs (e.g. a
// stream that never drained). Leaving them enforced surfaces real issues
// while this file silences only the ~95% pattern noise.
//
// Picked up via `errcheck -exclude .errcheck ./...` — pass the flag
// explicitly; errcheck does not auto-discover the file.
fmt.Fprint
fmt.Fprintf
fmt.Fprintln
